1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:08,320
Today, I'm speaking with Michael Nielsen. You have done many things. You're one of the pioneers of quantum computing, wrote the main textbook in the field of the open science movement.

2
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:15,760
You wrote a book about deep learning that Chris Olah and Greg Brockman credit with getting them into the field. More recently, you're a research fellow

3
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:25,120
at the Astera Institute and writing a book about religion, science, and technology. I'm going to ask you about none of those things. The conversation I want to have today is,

4
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:35,120
how do we recognize scientific progress? It's especially relevant for AI because people are trying to close the RL verification loop on scientific discovery.

5
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:46,400
What does it mean to close that loop? But in preparing for this interview, I've realized that it's a more mysterious and elusive force, even in the history of human science, than I understood.

6
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:58,800
I think a good place to start will be Michelson-Morley and how special relativity is discovered, if it's different from the story that you get off of YouTube videos.

7
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:09,920
I will prompt you that way, and then we'll go in there. Michelson-Morley is the famous result often presented as this experiment that was done in

8
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:21,760
the 1880s that helped Einstein come up with the special theory of relativity a little bit later, changing the way we think about space and time and our fundamental conception of those things.

9
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:32,000
And there's a big gap, I think, between the way Michelson and Morley and other people at the time thought about the experiment and certainly the way in which Einstein thought

10
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:42,880
or did not think about the experiment. In actual fact, he stated later in his life he wasn't even sure whether he was aware of the paper at the time.

11
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:55,840
There's a lot of evidence that he probably was aware of the paper at the time, but it actually wasn't dispositive for his thinking at all. Something else completely was going on.

12
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:05,280
What Michelson and Morley thought they were doing was testing different theories of what was called the ether. If you go back to the 1600s,

13
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:16,480
Robert Boyle introduced the idea of the ether. We know that sound is vibrations in the air. Boyle and other people got interested in the question of

14
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:24,480
whether light is vibrations in something, and they couldn't figure out what it was. Boyle did an experiment where he tested whether you could propagate

15
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:32,400
light through a vacuum. He found that you could. You couldn't do it with sound. He introduced this idea of the ether, and for the next two hundred or so years,

16
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:44,640
people had all these conversations about what the ether was and what its nature was. The Michelson and Morley experiment was really an experiment to test different theories of the ether

17
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:55,760
against one another, in particular to find out whether or not there was a so-called ether wind. The idea was that the Earth is maybe passing through this ether wind.

18
00:02:55,760 --> 00:03:08,000
And if it is passing through the ether wind and you shoot a light beam parallel to the direction the ether wind is going in, it'll get accelerated a little bit.

19
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,400
If it's being passed back in the opposite direction, it'll get slowed down a little bit, and you should be able to see this in the results of interference experiments.

20
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:26,720
What they found, much to their surprise, was that in fact there was no ether wind. That ruled out some theories of the ether, but not all, and Michelson

21
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:36,960
certainly continued to believe in the ether. This is what was a shocking part of reading this story from the biography of Einstein that you recommended by... what was his first name?

22
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:45,280
Abraham Pais. Abraham Pais. Subtle is the Lord. Also from Imre Lakatos, The Methodology of Scientific Research Programmes.

23
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:56,560
The way it's told is that Michelson-Morley proved that the ether did not exist. Therefore, it created a crisis in physics that Einstein solved with special relativity.

24
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:02,080
What you're pointing out is he actually was trying to distinguish between many different theories of ether. If you're in space or if you're on Earth,

25
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:08,960
it's the same direction of ether, or maybe the ether wind is being carried around by the Earth, and so you can't really experience it on Earth. But if you go to a high enough altitude,

26
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:17,360
you might be able to experience it. In fact, Michelson's experiments, the famous one is 1887, but he conducted these experiments for basically two decades.

27
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:26,720
For longer than that. He conducted the first one in 1881, I think, but he continued to believe until he died. He died, I think it was 1929 or so. It was the

28
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:38,080
late twenties. He was still doing experiments in the 1920s about whether or not the ether existed. So he continued to believe in the ether to the end of his life.

29
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:48,080
I think the last public statement he made was a year or two before he died, and he basically still believed it at that point. In fact, there was another physicist, Miller,

30
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:57,520
who kept doing these experiments in the 1920s. He thought that if he went to a high enough altitude, Mount Wilson in California… "Oh, I'm high enough that the ether

31
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:06,160
winds are not being dragged by the Earth. And I've measured the effect of the ether." Einstein hears about this and he says, and this is where you get the famous quote,

32
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:16,880
"Subtle is the Lord, but malicious He is not." Anyways, I think the reason the story is interesting is for many different reasons. One of the ways in which the real history of

33
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:29,040
science is different from this idea you get of the scientific method is that you really can't apply falsification as easily as you might think. It's not clear what is being falsified.

34
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:36,960
Is it just another version of the theory of the ether that's being falsified? Certainly you can't induce the theory of special relativity from the fact

35
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:47,600
that one version of the ether seems to be disconfirmed by these experiments. It certainly doesn't show that ideas about falsification are wrong or falsified,

36
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:58,720
but it does show that the most naive ideas… Things are often much more complicated than you think. Michelson did this experiment in 1881. He was a very young man, and then other people,

37
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:08,400
I think Rayleigh was one of them, pointed out that there were some problems with the way he did it, so they had to redo it in 1887. At that point, a lot of the leading

38
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:23,200
physicists of the day basically accepted this result, that there was no ether wind. But what to do about this? Sure, maybe you falsified some theories of the ether. There are others that you

39
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:34,160
haven't falsified at all at this point, and people set to work on developing those. It is funny, people will phrase it as showing that the ether didn't exist.

40
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:45,600
Even just the word "the" there is a misnomer. You actually had a ton of different theories and a couple of leading contenders. So yes, there's some version of

41
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:59,600
falsification going on, but how you respond to this new experiment is very complicated. Certainly the leading physicists of the day responded by saying, "Okay, this gives us a

42
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:10,240
lot of information about what the ether must be, but it doesn't tell us that there is no ether." In fact, Lorentz at the end of the 19th century, before Einstein,

43
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:20,640
figures out the math of how you convert from one reference frame to another reference frame, and comes up with the Lorentz transformations, which is the basis of special relativity.

44
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:30,560
But his interpretation is that you are converting from the ether reference frame to these non-privileged other reference frames if you're moving relative to the ether.

45
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:43,760
His interpretation of length contraction and time dilation is that this is the effect of moving through the ether, and you have this pressure. This pressure is warping clocks. It's warping

46
00:07:43,760 --> 00:08:00,240
measures of length. The interesting thing here is that experimentally you cannot distinguish Lorentz's interpretation from special relativity. I think that's a strong statement.

47
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:11,680
Lorentz introduces this quantity called local time, which he regards as... My understanding is he's not trying to give a physical interpretation of this,

48
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:20,480
but it's what Einstein would later just recognize as time in another inertial reference frame. He's not trying to attribute much physical meaning to it.

49
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:35,120
I think Poincaré gets much closer later on to realizing that this is the time that's registered by clocks. About forty-odd years later, people start

50
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:45,760
doing these muon experiments where they see cosmic rays hit the top of the atmosphere. They produce a shower of muons, and you can look to see at different heights in the atmosphere how

51
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:57,520
many of those muons remain. They decay over time, and a very strange thing happens, which is that they're decaying way too slow.

52
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:10,240
You expect they shouldn't be able to last the whole way through the atmosphere at all. Their decay rate is too quick, if you were in a classical theory.

53
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:21,200
But if in fact their time really has slowed down, it's okay. In fact, the measured decay rates in 1940—and there have since been more

54
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:34,000
accurate experiments done—match exactly what you expect from special relativity. That's the kind of thing where if Lorentz had been alive—he'd been dead ten or so years at that

55
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:50,880
point—it seems quite likely that he would have tried to save his theory by patching it up yet again, but it would have been a massive setback. It starts to just look like time—this

56
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:58,560
thing that Lorentz introduced as a mathematical convenience—that's actually what time is, for the muons at least. Then there's a whole bunch of other experiments

57
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:05,440
that show this very similar phenomenon. When was that experiment done? That was, I think, 1940. It might have been published in 1941.

58
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:17,520
Maybe to rephrase and change my claim: it's not that you could not have distinguished them, but the scientific community adopted what we in retrospect consider the more correct

59
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:29,520
interpretation before it was actually experimentally shown to be preferred. So there's clearly some process that human science does which can distinguish different theories.

60
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:43,280
Can I just interrupt? You used the word process, and it's interesting to think about that term. Process carries connotations of something set in advance.

61
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:57,760
It's much more complicated in practice. You have people like Lorentz, who Einstein absolutely and utterly admired, and Poincaré, one of the greatest scientists who ever lived,

62
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:08,240
and Michelson, another truly outstanding scientist, who never reconciled themselves. It's not as though there's some standard procedure that we're all using to reconcile these things.

63
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:18,000
Great scientists can remain wrong for a very long time after the scientific community has broadly changed its opinion. But there's no centralized

64
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:30,160
authority or centralized method. That is the interesting thing. There's progress even though it is hard to articulate the process by which it happens, the heuristics that

65
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:44,160
are used. You mentioned Poincaré. Lorentz has the math right, but the interpretation wrong. It seems like Poincaré had the opposite, where he understood that it's hard to define simultaneity

66
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:56,960
because it requires a circular definition with time, or velocity of something that might arrive at a midpoint together, but velocity is defined in terms of time. I find this interesting. There are

67
00:11:56,960 --> 00:12:07,040
a couple of other examples we could call on. There is this phenomenon in the history of science where somebody asks the right question, but then they don't clinch it.

68
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:18,800
I'm curious what you think is happening in those cases. You actually do want to go case by case and try to understand. It's not necessarily clear that they're doing the same thing wrong in all of the

69
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:28,240
cases. The Poincaré case is amazing. He seems to have understood the principle of relativity, the idea that the laws of physics are the same in all inertial reference frames.

70
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:36,480
He seems to have understood that the speed of light is the same in all inertial reference frames. He doesn't phrase it quite that way, but it is

71
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:49,600
my understanding, though I don't speak French. These are basically the ideas that Einstein uses to deduce special relativity. But then he also has this additional

72
00:12:49,600 --> 00:13:04,960
misunderstanding where he thinks that length contraction is a dynamical effect, that somehow particles are being pushed together by some external force, something is going on dynamically.

73
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:15,280
He doesn't understand that it's purely kinematics. That actually space and time are different from what we thought, and you need to fundamentally rethink those things.

74
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:29,200
It's almost like he knew too much. He had almost too grand a vision in mind. Einstein subtracts from that and says, "No. Space and time are just different than what we

75
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:44,800
thought, and here's the correct picture." There's a paper in, I think it's 1909, where Poincaré still has this dynamical picture of what's going on with the length contraction.

76
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:59,920
This is just not necessary. This is a mistake from the modern point of view. Why is he doing this? Why is he clinging onto this idea? I don't know. I've obviously never met the man.

77
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:13,840
It would be fascinating to be able to talk it over and try and understand. His expertise seems to be getting in the way. He knows so much, he understands so much,

78
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:26,720
and then he's not able to let go of these things. A really interesting fact is that a few years prior, in the 1890s, Einstein's a teenager and he believes in the ether too. He knows

79
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:38,960
about this stuff. But he's not quite as attached as these older people were. Maybe they were a little bit prisoners of their own expertise. That's my guess. Some

80
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:53,680
historians of science would certainly disagree. Then there's the obvious stories where Einstein himself later on is said to have not latched onto the correct interpretations of quantum mechanics

81
00:14:53,680 --> 00:15:04,480
or cosmology because of his own attachments. Yeah. Here’s the bigger question I have. The muon example is a great example of

82
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:15,840
these long verification loops and how progress seems to happen in the scientific community faster than these verification loops imply. Maybe the clearest example is Aristarchus

83
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:24,320
in the second century BC comes up with the idea of heliocentrism. The ancient Athenians dismiss it on the grounds that we should see as the Earth

84
