112
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:09,760
That's almost definitionally what causes the bottlenecks. Because people are smart, they know what has worked before. They study it. They apply

113
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:22,640
the same kinds of things, so they don't get stuck in the same places as before. They keep getting bottlenecked in different places. I'm overgeneralizing a bit, but I think it's right.

114
00:21:22,640 --> 00:21:31,760
If you're attempting to reduce science to a process, you're attempting to reduce it to something where there is just a method which you can apply, and you

115
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:43,760
turn the crank and out pops insight. You can do a certain amount of that, but you're going to get bottlenecked at the places where your existing method doesn't apply.

116
00:21:43,760 --> 00:21:57,520
Definitionally, there's no crank you can turn. You need a lot of people trying different ideas. The more difficult the idea is to have, the greater the bottleneck,

