00:53:57,920 --> 00:54:06,640
We'll still be subject to the laws of physics, but there is this tremendous freedom in there. This looks to me like we're down at the bottom of the tech tree.

288
00:54:06,640 --> 00:54:18,480
We've barely gotten started there, and I expect that to be the case broadly. Certainly, programming is a very natural place to look.

289
00:54:18,480 --> 00:54:34,480
The idea that we've discovered all the deep ideas in programming just seems obviously ludicrous. We keep discovering what seem like deep, new, fundamental ideas. We're very limited. We're

290
00:54:34,480 --> 00:54:51,440
basically slightly jumped-up chimpanzees, so we're slow and it's taking us time. But what do we look like another million years in the future, in terms of all the

291
00:54:51,440 --> 00:55:07,520
different ideas people have had around how to manipulate computers and information? I think we're likely to discover that there are a lot of very deep ideas still to be discovered.

292
00:55:07,520 --> 00:55:18,320
I think it was Knuth in the preface to The Art of Computer Programming who says something like it. He started this book back in the sixties. He talked to a mathematician who was a bit

293
00:55:18,320 --> 00:55:26,480
contemptuous and said, "Look, computer science isn't really a thing yet. Come back to me when there's a thousand deep theorems."

294
00:55:26,480 --> 00:55:41,040
Knuth remarks, writing the preface decades later, "There clearly are a thousand deep theorems now." It's really interesting to think what the long-term future is as you get higher and

295
00:55:41,040 --> 00:55:56,720
higher up in the tech tree, choices about which direction we go and how we choose to explore. It's potentially the case that different civilizations or different choices mean

296
00:55:56,720 --> 00:56:10,640
we end up in different parts of that tree. In particular, there are just very basic things about how we're very visual creatures, while certain other animals are much more aurally based.

297
00:56:10,640 --> 00:56:23,200
Does that bias the types of thoughts that you have? Then you extend it to much more exotic kinds of civilizations where maybe their biases in

298
00:56:23,200 --> 00:56:35,280
terms of how they perceive and manipulate the world are quite different than ours. That might make some significant changes in terms of how they do

299
00:56:35,280 --> 00:56:43,920
that exploration of the tech tree. It's all speculation, obviously. This is such an interesting take. I want to better understand it.

300
00:56:43,920 --> 00:56:51,120
One way to understand it is that there might be some things which are so fundamental and have such a wide collision area against reality that they're inevitably

301
00:56:51,120 --> 00:57:04,640
going to discover, like general relativity. Numbers. Numbers. Of all the intelligences in the Milky Way galaxy… Maybe that number is one. Well, actually, arguably we've already

302
00:57:04,640 --> 00:57:17,040
increased the number. But of all of those, what fraction have the concept of counting? It does seem very natural. What fraction have

303
00:57:17,040 --> 00:57:30,000
discovered the idea of some kind of decimal place system? Interesting question. Maybe we're missing something really simple and obvious that's actually way better than that. What fraction

304
00:57:30,000 --> 00:57:39,280
got there immediately? What fraction had to go through some other intermediate state? What fraction uses linear representations versus a two-dimensional or a

305
00:57:39,280 --> 00:57:45,840
three-dimensional representation? I think the answers to these questions are just not at all obvious. It's a lot of design freedom.

306
00:57:45,840 --> 00:58:02,160
On theoretical computer science, this is going to be extremely naive and arrogant, but I took Scott Aaronson's class on complexity theory, and I was by far the worst student he's ever had.

307
00:58:02,160 --> 00:58:13,760
What I remember is there was this period, in which you were one of the pioneers, where we figured out the class of problems that quantum computers can solve and how it

308
00:58:13,760 --> 00:58:22,960
relates to problems that classical computers can solve. It was groundbreaking. It's crazy that this works. Since then… There's literally this website called Complexity

309
00:58:22,960 --> 00:58:34,480
Zoo which lists out all the complexity classes. If you have this complexity class with this kind of oracle, it's equivalent to this other class. It feels like we're building out that taxonomy.

310
00:58:34,480 --> 00:58:39,120
There are a couple ways to understand what you're saying. One, maybe you disagree with me that this is actually what's happened with this field.

311
00:58:39,120 --> 00:58:49,920
Another is that while that might happen to any one field, who would've thought in 1880 that computer science, other than Babbage, was going to be a thing in the first place?

312
00:58:49,920 --> 00:59:03,680
We're underestimating how many more fields there could be. Or maybe you think both, or maybe a third secret thing. I'd be curious. A very common argument here is the low-hanging fruit argument.

313
00:59:03,680 --> 00:59:16,960
The argument that says there should be diminishing returns. In fact, empirically we see this. The amount of scientists in the world has exponentially increased. I think it's worth thinking about why

314
00:59:16,960 --> 00:59:30,880
you expect diminishing returns and how well that argument actually applies in practice. An analogy I like is thinking about going to an event, like a wedding,

315
00:59:30,880 --> 00:59:43,120
and you go to the dessert buffet. They've put out thirty desserts. Naturally, what people do is the best desserts go first. We don't quite have a well-ordered preference

316
00:59:43,120 --> 00:59:53,440
there, so maybe there's some difference, but human beings are fairly similar, so the best desserts will go first. This is an argument for why you expect

317
00:59:53,440 --> 01:00:03,200
diminishing returns in a lot of different fields. If it's relatively easy to see what's available and people have similar preferences, then the best stuff goes first and it

318
01:00:03,200 --> 01:00:16,640
just gets worse and worse after that. If you look at a very static snapshot in time of scientific progress, maybe there's some truth to that.

319
01:00:16,640 --> 01:00:27,280
But if somebody is standing behind the dessert table and is replenishing and restocking the desserts and keeps adding new ones in, it may turn out that a little bit later,

320
01:00:27,280 --> 01:00:36,640
much better desserts appear, and you're going to go and eat those instead. Scientific progress has a little bit of that flavor.

321
01:00:36,640 --> 01:00:48,720
We go through these funny time periods. Computer science is a great example, where computer science basically arose as a side effect of some pretty abstruse questions

322
01:00:48,720 --> 01:01:04,240
in the philosophy of mathematics and logic. You've got these people trying to attack these rather esoteric questions that seem quite high up in exploration,

323
01:01:04,240 --> 01:01:12,480
and they discover this fundamental new field, and all of a sudden there's an explosion there. The diminishing returns argument just didn't apply there.

324
01:01:12,480 --> 01:01:24,400
We just weren't able to see what was there. This has been the case over and over again. New fields arrive and all of a sudden, and boom, it's easy to make progress again.

325
01:01:24,400 --> 01:01:32,000
Young people flood in because you can be twenty-one and make major breakthroughs rather than having to spend twenty-five years mastering everything that's been

326
01:01:32,000 --> 01:01:47,040
done before. It's obviously very attractive. I'm not sure anybody understands very well the dynamics of that, or how to think about why the structure of knowledge is that way,

327
01:01:47,040 --> 01:01:58,880
where these new fields keep opening up. But it does seem empirically to be the case. Despite the fact that that is the case… Take deep learning.

328
01:01:58,880 --> 01:02:08,960
Obviously, this is an example of a new field where twenty-one-year-olds can make progress and it's relatively new. Fifteen years or so

329
01:02:08,960 --> 01:02:20,000
since it got back into high gear. But already we're in a stage where you need billions, tens of billions, or hundreds of billions of dollars

330
01:02:20,000 --> 01:02:28,640
to keep making progress at the frontier. There are a couple ways to understand that. One is that it actually is harder than the kinds of things the ancients had to do,

331
01:02:28,640 --> 01:02:37,360
or is more intensive at least. Second is it might not have been, but because our civilizational resources are so large, the amount of people is so large,

332
01:02:37,360 --> 01:02:47,440
the amount of money is so large, we can basically make the kind of progress it would have taken the ancients forever to make almost immediately. We notice something is productive and immediately

333
01:02:47,440 --> 01:02:55,440
dump in all the resources. But it's also weird that there's not that many of them. I feel like deep learning is notable

334
01:02:55,440 --> 01:03:03,760
because it is one big exception to the fact that it's hard to think of other examples. I think that's a consequence of the architecture of attention.

335
01:03:03,760 --> 01:03:12,000
At any given time, there's always a most successful thing. If deep learning wasn't a thing, maybe you'd be talking about CRISPR.

336
01:03:12,000 --> 01:03:28,240
Maybe we wouldn't think about solving the protein structure prediction problem as a success of AI. Maybe we would have figured out how to do it with curve fitting, more broadly construed,

337
01:03:28,240 --> 01:03:36,720
and we'd just be like, "Wow, that took a lot of computing resources." But protein structure prediction might be an enormously important thing.

338
01:03:36,720 --> 01:03:49,840
There is always our biggest thing. What you're pointing at is more a consequence of the way in which attention gets centralized. It's basically fashion, is what I'm saying.

339
01:03:49,840 --> 01:04:05,440
It's not just fashion, but there is some dynamic there. There's a very interesting and important implication of this idea. That the branching is so wide and so contingent and so path-dependent that

340
01:04:05,440 --> 01:04:13,040
different civilizations would stumble on entirely different technology stacks. There's a very interesting implication that there will be gains from trade into the far,

341
01:04:13,040 --> 01:04:22,800
far future, which might actually be one of the most important facts about the far future in terms of how civilizations are set up, how they coordinate, and how they interface.

342
01:04:22,800 --> 01:04:40,160
There's not this "go forth and exploit." There are humongous gains to trade from adjacent colonies or whatever. Sort of. There's a question of what's actually hard. If it's just the ideas,

343
01:04:40,160 --> 01:04:48,080
well, those spread relatively quickly. It's relatively easy to share ideas. If it's something more, it's almost a Dan Wang kind of idea

344
01:04:48,080 --> 01:04:59,760
where there's some notion of capacity. You need all the right techs, you need all the right manufacturing capacity, and so on. So civilization A has a very different kind

345
01:04:59,760 --> 01:05:08,240
of manufacturing capacity, and it's just not so easy to build in civilization B. Even if civilization B is ahead, I think that becomes true.

346
01:05:08,240 --> 01:05:23,440
There is a comparative advantage which is going to provide massive benefits to trade in both directions. Eventually, you expect some diffusion of innovation. It is funny to think about

347
01:05:23,440 --> 01:05:36,400
what the barriers are there. A fun thought experiment I like to think about is GitHub but for aliens. Somebody presents you with all of the code

348
01:05:36,400 --> 01:05:50,080
from some alien civilization. I don't even know what code means there, but their specification of algorithms. It would have many interesting new ideas in there,

349
01:05:50,080 --> 01:06:05,120
and it would take forever for human beings to dig through and try and extract all of those. The origin of this for me was thinking about proteins in nature.

350
01:06:05,120 --> 01:06:17,200
We've been gifted this incredible variety of machines which we don't really understand at all. We just have to go and try and understand them on a one-by-one basis.

351
01:06:17,200 --> 01:06:28,000
We're still understanding hemoglobin and insulin and things like this. There are hundreds of millions of proteins known. So it is a little bit like that.

352
01:06:28,000 --> 01:06:43,360
We've been gifted by biology this immense library of machines, no doubt containing an enormous number of very interesting ideas, and we're just at the very, very beginning of understanding it.

353
01:06:43,360 --> 01:06:56,560
I suppose your point—I need to relabel your argument slightly—but you think of that as a gift from an alien civilization, which obviously it isn't, but you think of it that way.

354
01:06:56,560 --> 01:07:04,960
And oh my goodness, there's so much in there and we're going to study it. Goodness knows how long we could continue to study it.

355
01:07:04,960 --> 01:07:13,760
There are tens of thousands of papers about hemoglobin and things like that, and we still don't understand them, and yet we're getting so much out of it. Just think about

356
01:07:13,760 --> 01:07:27,680
insulin alone. It's such an important thing. That's an incredibly useful intuition pump, that you have on Earth… I had Nick Lane on where he had this theory about how life emerged,

357
01:07:27,680 --> 01:07:37,440
but whatever theory you have, something like DNA has had four billion years. You have an alien civilization come here and be like, "There's all these interesting

358
01:07:37,440 --> 01:07:52,320
things to learn about material science." Think about kinesin walking along. We know almost nothing about these proteins, and yet the tiny few facts we do know are just incredible.

359
01:07:52,320 --> 01:08:09,760
The ribosome is another example, this miraculous sort of device, a little factory. All seeded by this particular chemistry on Earth with nucleic acids and carbon-based life forms.

360
01:08:09,760 --> 01:08:19,680
That chemistry gives rise to all of these interesting things which an alien civilization would find very interesting. That very seed, which must be one among trillions

361
01:08:19,680 --> 01:08:29,439
of possible seeds of general intellectual ideas, leads to all this fecundity. That's a very interesting intuition pump. I want to meditate on this "gains from trade"

362
01:08:29,439 --> 01:08:39,279
thing because I feel like there's something very interesting about this idea that if you have this vision of how technology progresses and how it may be different in different civilizations,

363
01:08:39,279 --> 01:08:45,200
it actually has important implications about how different civilizations might interact with each other. The fact that there are going

364
01:08:45,200 --> 01:08:54,399
to be these huge gains from trade. It makes friendliness much more rewarding? Yes. That's a very important observation. I hadn't thought about that at all.

365
01:08:54,399 --> 01:09:16,720
That is a very interesting observation. It is funny. Comparative advantage is something that people love to invoke and it's a very beautiful idea obviously. There are limits to it.

366
01:09:16,720 --> 01:09:31,120
It's a special limited model. Chimpanzees can do interesting things, but we don't trade with them. I think it's interesting to think about the reasons why.

367
01:09:31,120 --> 01:09:44,399
Part of it is just power, I think. Once there's a sufficiently large power imbalance, very often—not always, but very often—groups of people seem to shift into this other

368
01:09:44,399 --> 01:09:57,680
mode where they just seek to dominate. Maybe there's something special about human beings, but maybe it's also a more general thing. You need all these special things to be

369
01:09:57,680 --> 01:10:09,760
true before groups will trade. It's not necessarily obvious. I think the big thing going on here is one, transaction costs.

370
01:10:09,760 --> 01:10:22,880
Two, comparative advantage does not tell you that the terms on which the trade happens are above subsistence for any given producer. People often bring this up in the context of,

371
01:10:22,880 --> 01:10:32,400
"Well, humans will be employed even in a post-AGI world because of comparative advantage." There are five different ways that argument breaks down, but the easiest way to understand it is:

372
01:10:32,400 --> 01:10:40,880
why don't we have horses all around on the roads? Because there's some comparative advantage between cars and horses. One, there are huge

373
01:10:40,880 --> 01:10:52,080
transaction costs to building roads that are compatible with horses and cars at the same time. In a similar way, AI thinking at 1,000 times the speed that can shoot their latent states at each

374
01:10:52,080 --> 01:11:07,120
other is going to find it way more costly than the benefit, in terms of interacting with a human being in the supply chain. Second, just because horses have a comparative

375
01:11:07,120 --> 01:11:19,600
advantage mathematically does not mean that it is worth paying $100,000 a year, or whatever it costs to sustain a horse in San Francisco. That subsistence isn't going to be worth the

376
01:11:19,600 --> 01:11:32,080
benefit you get out of the horse. I do think it's interesting, the sheer fact… My expectation and my intuition obviously differs a great deal from yours on this.

377
01:11:32,080 --> 01:11:38,240
Most parts of the tech tree are never going to be explored. There are just too many interesting ways of combining things.

378
01:11:38,240 --> 01:11:50,160
There are too many deep ideas waiting to be discovered, and not only we, but nobody ever is going to discover most of them. So choices about how to do the

379
01:11:50,160 --> 01:11:59,200
exploration actually matter quite a bit. It's something I really dislike about technological determinist arguments. I'm willing to buy it low enough

380
01:11:59,200 --> 01:12:12,000
down when progress is relatively simple. But higher up, you start to get to shape the way in which you do the exploration. And it's interesting, we are starting to

381
01:12:12,000 --> 01:12:22,960
shape it in interesting ways. There are various technologies that have been essentially banned. You think about DDT, chlorofluorocarbons,

382
01:12:22,960 --> 01:12:36,560
restrictions on the use of nuclear weapons, the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty. Those kinds of things weren't done before the fact, but they're starting to get pretty close

383
01:12:36,560 --> 01:12:47,440
in some cases, where we just preemptively decide, "Oh, we're not going to go down that path." So that starts to look like a set of institutions where we are actually

384
01:12:47,440 --> 01:12:59,200
influencing how we explore the tech tree. On where you would see these gains from trade, obviously you'd see the most where it's pure information that could be sent back and forth,

385
01:12:59,200 --> 01:13:09,120
because the information has this quality where it is expensive to produce, but cheap to verify and cheap to send. It'll be interesting how much of future

386
01:13:09,120 --> 01:13:18,080
productivity can be distilled down to information. Right now, it's hard to do. If China's really good at manufacturing something, there's this process knowledge

387
01:13:18,080 --> 01:13:26,080
that's in the heads of 100 million people involved in the manufacturing sector in China. But in the future, it might be easier if AIs are doing it.

388
01:13:26,080 --> 01:13:39,920
The question is to what extent our fabrication gets very uniform and gets really commoditized. 3D printers have been the next big thing for at least 20 years now.

389
01:13:39,920 --> 01:13:50,000
Why do they still not work all that well? Why are they still not at the center of manufacturing, and what comes after that? It is funny to look at the ribosome by contrast,

390
01:13:50,000 --> 01:14:00,480
which really is at the center of biology in a whole lot of really interesting ways. Whether or not that's the future of manufacturing is something very simple,

391
01:14:00,480 --> 01:14:15,600
where everything goes as throughput through a bioreactor or something like that. You send the information, and then you grow stuff, or you have some 3D printer that actually works.

392
01:14:15,600 --> 01:14:27,120
If they're good enough, then it does become much more a pure information problem, and some of this process knowledge becomes much less important. Jane Street has a lot of compute, but GPUs are

393
01:14:27,120 --> 01:14:36,240
very expensive, and so even optimizations that have a relatively small effect on GPU utilization are still extremely valuable. Two of Jane Street's ML engineers,

394
01:14:36,240 --> 01:14:42,800
Corwin and Sylvain, walked through some of their optimization workflows at GTC. You're not bottlenecked on the network being too slow, you're bottlenecked on

395
01:14:42,800 --> 01:14:51,600
waiting for a different rank in your training not having completed the work. They talked about how Jane Street profiles traces and diagnoses bottlenecks, and then

396
01:14:51,600 --> 01:14:59,280
how they solve them using techniques like CUDA graphs and CUDA streams and custom kernels. With these sorts of optimizations, Corwin and Sylvain were able to

397
01:14:59,280 --> 01:15:08,400
get their training steps down from 400 milliseconds to 375 milliseconds each. This 25 millisecond difference might sound small, but given the size of Jane Street's fleet,

398
01:15:08,400 --> 01:15:17,840
that improvement could free up thousands of B200s. Jane Street open sourced all the relevant code. If you want to check it out, I've linked the GitHub repo and the talk in the description below.

399
01:15:17,840 --> 01:15:29,120
And if you find this stuff exciting, Jane Street is hiring researchers and engineers. Go to janestreet.com/dwarkesh to learn more. Can I ask a very clumsily phrased question?

400
01:15:29,120 --> 01:15:39,840
There are these deep principles that we've discovered a couple of. One is this idea that if there's a symmetry across a dimension, it corresponds to a conserved

401
01:15:39,840 --> 01:15:52,160
quantity. It's a very deep idea. There's another—which you've written a lot about, written a textbook about in fact—about ways to understand what kinds of things you can compute,

402
01:15:52,160 --> 01:16:01,920
what kinds of physical systems you can understand with other physical systems, what a universal computer looks like, et cetera. Is your view that if you go down to this level of

403
01:16:01,920 --> 01:16:11,920
idea of Noether's theorem or the Church-Turing principle, that there's an infinite number of extremely deep such principles? Because I feel what makes them special

404
01:16:11,920 --> 01:16:23,920
is that they themselves encompass so many different possible ways the world could be. But no, the world has to be compatible with a couple of these very deep principles.

405
01:16:23,920 --> 01:16:33,200
I don't know. All I have here is speculation and instinct. My instinct is that we keep finding very fundamental new things.

406
01:16:33,200 --> 01:16:46,640
It was quite formative for me to understand, as I gave the example before, these wonderful ideas of Church and Turing and these other people about universal programmable devices.

407
01:16:46,640 --> 01:16:55,120
Then you understand later, this also contains within it the ideas of public-key cryptography. Then you understand later, that also contains within it

408
01:16:55,120 --> 01:17:11,200
the ideas people refer to as cryptocurrency. There's a very deep set of ideas there about the ability to collectively maintain an agreed-upon ledger, which is built upon this.

409
01:17:11,200 --> 01:17:28,320
It's taken many years to figure out the right canonical form of those. Just this fact that you keep finding what seem like deep new fundamental primitives

410
01:17:28,320 --> 01:17:37,920
has been a very important intuition pump for me. I've given that particular example, but I think you see that same pattern in a lot of different areas.

411
01:17:37,920 --> 01:17:50,080
What is your interpretation then of this empirical phenomenon where whatever input you consider into the scientific process or technological progress… Economists have studied this a million ways.

412
01:17:50,080 --> 01:18:00,560
It just seems to require a very consistent rate of X percent more researchers per year. There's this famous paper from a couple years ago by Nicholas Bloom and others where they say,

413
01:18:00,560 --> 01:18:08,400
"How many people are working in the semiconductor industry, and how has it increased over time through the history of Moore's law?" I think they find that Moore's law

414
01:18:08,400 --> 01:18:19,040
means transistor density increases 40% a year, but to keep that going the number of scientists has increased 9% a year, in the semiconductor industry.

415
01:18:19,040 --> 01:18:25,920
They go through industry after industry with this observation. Is your view that there are these deep ideas, but they keep getting harder to find?

416
01:18:25,920 --> 01:18:37,920
Or is there another way to think about what's happening with these empirical observations? First of all, all of their examples are narrow. They pick a particular thing, and then they

417
01:18:37,920 --> 01:19:02,160
look at a particular metric. GPUs don't show up there. All of a sudden you get this ability to parallelize, and that's really interesting. There are a lot of external consequences.

418
01:19:02,160 --> 01:19:14,880
Basically they have these simple quantitative measures. They look at it in agricultural productivity. They look at it in a whole lot of different ways, but you do have to focus narrowly.

419
01:19:14,880 --> 01:19:33,520
I'm certainly interested in the fact that new types of progress keep becoming possible. But I think even there, there does still seem to be some phenomenon of diminishing returns.

420
01:19:33,520 --> 01:19:44,560
Is that intrinsic? Is that something about the structure of the world? What is it? One thing which hasn't changed that much is the individual minds which are doing this kind of work.

421
01:19:44,560 --> 01:20:02,720
Maybe those should be improved as well, or some feedback process going on there. Maybe that changes the nature of things. I look at scientific progress up until,

422
01:20:02,720 --> 01:20:16,720
let's say, 1700, and it was very slow, and also very irregular. You had the Ionians back five centuries before Christ doing these quite remarkable things, and so

423
01:20:16,720 --> 01:20:28,080
much knowledge would get lost, and then it would be rediscovered, and then it would be lost again. You'd have to say that progress was very slow. It's partially just bound up with the fact

424
01:20:28,080 --> 01:20:35,440
that there were some very good ideas that we just didn't have. Even once you've had the ideas, you need to build institutions around them.

425
01:20:35,440 --> 01:20:44,000
You actually need to solve a whole lot of different problems about training, allocation of capital, and all these kinds of things. Even just basic security for researchers,

426
01:20:44,000 --> 01:20:51,280
so they're not worried about the Inquisition or things like that. There are all these complicated problems. You solve all those complicated problems,

427
01:20:51,280 --> 01:21:02,800
and then all of a sudden, boom, there's a massive burst of scientific progress. If there's some kind of stagnation, if you're not changing those external circumstances, yes,

428
01:21:02,800 --> 01:21:14,800
you may start to get diminishing returns again. But that doesn't mean there's anything intrinsic about the situation. Maybe something external needs to change again.

429
01:21:14,800 --> 01:21:24,240
Obviously, a lot of people think AI is potentially going to be a driver. It certainly will at some level. To that extent, you can think of a lot

430
01:21:24,240 --> 01:21:37,120
of modern scientific instrumentation as really, at some level, robots. What is the James Webb Space Telescope? It's unconventional maybe to describe

431
01:21:37,120 --> 01:21:47,520
it as a robot, but it's not completely unreasonable either. It is an example of a highly automated, very sophisticated system with electronically

432
01:21:47,520 --> 01:21:58,240
mediated sensors and actuators, where machine learning is being used to process the data. In that sense, we're already starting to see that transition.

433
01:21:58,240 --> 01:22:06,320
We've been seeing it for decades. I have this "smoke a joint and take a puff" thought, which— I think we've had a few.

434
01:22:06,320 --> 01:22:14,560
I think we're getting to that part of the conversation, and then you can help me get my foot out of my mouth and figure out a more concrete way to think about it.

435
01:22:14,560 --> 01:22:26,560
To your point that there was the Industrial Revolution, the Enlightenment, and now there's AI, and each might be a different pace or a different way in which science happens.

436
01:22:26,560 --> 01:22:39,120
If you think about the pace of how fast such transitions have been happening, you can draw over the long span of human history this hyperbolic rate of growth that is

437
01:22:39,120 --> 01:22:46,080
increasing over time as well. A hundred thousand years ago, you had the Stone Age. You go back even much further, how long have primates been around?

438
01:22:46,080 --> 01:22:54,240
It would be millions of years. A hundred thousand years ago, the Stone Age, then ten thousand years ago, the Agricultural Revolution, then three hundred years ago,

439
01:22:54,240 --> 01:23:04,320
the Industrial Revolution, each marked by this increase in the rate of exponential growth. Then people think it's going to happen again with AI.

440
01:23:04,320 --> 01:23:12,400
But that would happen potentially even faster. It would not have occurred to somebody at the beginning of the Industrial Revolution that the next demarcation

441
01:23:12,400 --> 01:23:25,520
in this trend will be artificial intelligence. So if things are getting faster, and it's hard to anticipate what the next transition will be. I guess we just think of this singularity between

442
01:23:25,520 --> 01:23:36,160
now and AI as what distinguishes the past from the future. But applying the same heuristic that many people in the past should have had,

443
01:23:36,160 --> 01:23:46,560
maybe the "Intelligence Age" is also quite short and the next thing after that, we don't even have the ontology to describe what it is, the future will not think of the

444
01:23:46,560 --> 01:24:01,440
past as pre-intelligent AI and post-AI. No, obviously we can't prove this, but it certainly seems quite plausible. Part of the issue is just that the substrate

445
01:24:01,440 --> 01:24:20,160
we have available to conceive seems all wrong. You can't speculate with a bunch of chimpanzees about what it would be to have language. Just to pick a major transition in the past,

446
01:24:20,160 --> 01:24:34,720
the transition itself is the thing. It seems likely. If we're talking about "taking a puff" kind of thoughts, I'm certainly amused by the idea that there's going to be some

447
01:24:34,720 --> 01:24:45,360
transition involving artificial general intelligence using classical computers. But actually, there'll be an interesting transition with quantum computers as well.

448
01:24:45,360 --> 01:24:59,520
They're probably capable of a strictly larger class of potentially interesting computations. So maybe the character of AQGI, or whatever it should be called,

449
01:24:59,520 --> 01:25:12,960
is actually qualitatively different. So maybe there's a brief period between those two things. As I say, this is just speculation, but it's certainly amusing. Is there a reason to think that?

450
01:25:12,960 --> 01:25:24,320
From what I understand, for decades people like you have put pretty tight bounds on the kinds of things quantum computers are going to do. It'll speed up search somewhat.

451
01:25:24,320 --> 01:25:32,000
The kinds of things it speeds up extremely, like Shor's algorithm, it seems like… Again, maybe this is to your point that we can't predict in advance what's down the tech tree, but at least

452
01:25:32,000 --> 01:25:43,280
from here, it seems like you break encryption, but what else are you using Shor's algorithm to do? We've only been thinking about it for 40 or so years.

453
01:25:43,280 --> 01:25:56,720
Not for very long, and we haven't thought that hard about it as a civilization. Does it turn out that it's very narrow? Maybe. Does it turn out that it's very broad?

454
01:25:56,720 --> 01:26:06,800
That's also a really radical expansion that seems distinctly possible. Keep in mind as well, we've been doing it without the benefit of having the devices.

455
01:26:06,800 --> 01:26:16,400
That's a pretty big bottleneck to have. If you're thinking about computer science in the 1700s and you're like, "it can do AND/OR, what can come out of that?" You can't anticipate

456
01:26:16,400 --> 01:26:30,240
Bitcoin. You can't anticipate deep learning. Maybe you could if you were sufficiently bright, but it is a pretty hard situation. What is your inside view, having been

457
01:26:30,240 --> 01:26:40,000
in and contributing to quantum information and quantum computing back in the '90s and 2000s? What is your telling of the history of what was the bottleneck?

458
01:26:40,000 --> 01:26:53,280
What was the key transition that made it a real field? How do you rank the contributions from Feynman to Deutsch to everybody else who came along?

459
01:26:53,280 --> 01:27:04,800
Let's just focus on the question about what actually changed. Why was quantum computing not a thing in the 1950s? It could have been. Somebody

460
01:27:04,800 --> 01:27:14,800
like John von Neumann is a good example. He was absolutely pioneering computation. He also wrote a very important book about quantum mechanics and was deeply interested in it.

461
01:27:14,800 --> 01:27:21,440
He could have invented quantum computing at that time, and I think there were quite a number of people who potentially could have.

462
01:27:21,440 --> 01:27:31,440
So why do we have these papers by people like Feynman and Deutsch in the '80s? Those are fairly regarded as the foundation of the field.

463
01:27:31,440 --> 01:27:46,000
There are some partial anticipations a little bit earlier, but they were nowhere near as comprehensive and nowhere near as deep. You should ask David. You can't ask Feynman, unfortunately,

464
01:27:46,000 --> 01:27:58,480
but he'll know much better than I do. A couple things that I think are interesting. One is that computation became far more salient in the late '70s and early '80s.

465
01:27:58,480 --> 01:28:06,720
It just became a thing which many more people were interested in, partially for very banal reasons. You could go and buy a PC. You could buy an Apple II.

466
01:28:06,720 --> 01:28:14,960
You could buy a Commodore 64. You could buy all these kinds of things. It became apparent to people that these were very powerful devices, very interesting to think about.

467
01:28:14,960 --> 01:28:26,480
At the same time, in the quantum case, that was also the time of the Paul trap and the ability to trap single ions. Up to that point, we hadn't really had the

468
01:28:26,480 --> 01:28:41,440
ability to manipulate single quantum states. You got these two separate things that for historically contingent reasons had both matured around 1980 or so.

469
01:28:41,440 --> 01:28:55,120
Somebody like von Neumann could have had the idea earlier, but it is quite an interesting factor. There's a story about Richard Feynman. He went and got one of the

470
01:28:55,120 --> 01:29:16,000
first PCs around 1980 or 1981. He was apparently so excited with this device, he actually tripped and hurt himself quite badly carrying his brand-new computing device.

471
01:29:16,000 --> 01:29:32,400
That's a very historically contingent coincidence, having somebody who's very talented and understanding of quantum mechanics also just very excited about these new machines.

472
01:29:32,400 --> 01:29:47,120
It's not so surprising perhaps that he's thinking about it then. What similar story could you have told 10 years earlier? The conditions don't exist for it. I mean, it's quite a banal story, but…

473
01:29:47,120 --> 01:29:58,160
One of the things we were going to discuss was this idea you had about the market for follow-ups. I think this is the perfect story to discuss it for because you wrote the

474
01:29:58,160 --> 01:30:12,480
textbook about the field. "Mike and Ike" is the definitive textbook on quantum information. You presumably came in after Deutsch. But you in the '90s somehow identified

475
01:30:12,480 --> 01:30:20,480
it as the thing that is worth following up on and building on. Instead of talking about it more abstractly, I'd love to just hear the firsthand story

476
01:30:20,480 --> 01:30:30,720
of how you knew that this is the thing to do. Of all the things that were happening in physics and computing, how did you decide you want to think about this problem?

477
01:30:30,720 --> 01:30:44,720
Richard Feynman writes this great paper in 1982. David Deutsch writes an absolutely fantastic paper in 1985 sketching out a lot of the fundamental ideas of quantum computing. I'm

478
01:30:44,720 --> 01:30:56,800
11 in 1985. I'm not thinking about this. I'm playing soccer and doing whatever. But in 1992, I took a class on quantum mechanics that was really terrific, given by Gerard Milburn.

479
01:30:56,800 --> 01:31:12,320
I just went and asked Gerard one day after the fifth lecture or something. I said, "Do you have any papers or whatever that you could give me?" He said, "Come by my office in a couple of days' time."

480
01:31:12,320 --> 01:31:24,720
I did, and he presented me with a giant stack of papers, which included the Deutsch paper, the Feynman paper, and a whole bunch of other very fundamental papers about quantum computing

481
01:31:24,720 --> 01:31:39,880
and quantum information at a time when essentially nobody in the world was working on it. He was. I think he wrote the very first paper that proposed a practical approach to quantum computing.

482
01:31:39,880 --> 01:31:57,040
It wasn't very practical, but it was actually in a real system. So in some sense, I'm benefiting from the taste of this other person. As soon as I read the papers… These are exciting papers.

483
01:31:57,040 --> 01:32:06,480
They're asking very fundamental questions, and you realize I can make progress here. These are things that one could potentially work on.

484
01:32:06,480 --> 01:32:28,560
Deutsch has this conjecture, or thesis or whatever you’d call it, that a universal model, a quantum Turing machine, should be capable of efficiently simulating any physical system at all.

485
01:32:28,560 --> 01:32:39,680
This is a very provocative idea. I think in that paper, he more or less claims that he's proved it. I'm not sure everybody would agree with that.

486
01:32:39,680 --> 01:32:52,160
There are questions about whether or not you can simulate quantum field theory effectively. That kind of question is very interesting and very exciting.

487
01:32:52,160 --> 01:33:04,400
It's obviously a fundamental question about the universe. He has some wonderful ideas in there about quantum algorithms, where they come from,

488
01:33:04,400 --> 01:33:16,080
what they mean, and what they relate to the meaning of the wave function. Questions like this are still not agreed upon amongst physicists.

489
01:33:16,080 --> 01:33:26,160
There's just some sense of, "Oh, I am in contact with something which is (A) deeply important, and (B) we as a civilization don't have this." Of course, you start to focus

490
01:33:26,160 --> 01:33:44,080
your attention a little bit there. I'm not sure I got the answer to the question… Maybe I misunderstood the question. Maybe I'll explain the motivation first.

491
01:33:44,080 --> 01:33:57,840
In a previous conversation, we were discussing how you could have known in the 1940s that the Shannon theorems and Shannon's way of thinking about a communication channel is a deep idea that goes

492
01:33:57,840 --> 01:34:09,840
beyond the problems with pulse-code modulation that Bell Labs was trying to solve at the time, and that it applies to everything from quantum mechanics to genetics to computer science.

493
01:34:09,840 --> 01:34:19,520
One of the ideas you stated that we didn't get a chance to talk about yet… Shannon published this paper. There are all these other papers,

494
01:34:19,520 --> 01:34:29,760
but there's some market of follow-ups where people gravitate to and build upon Shannon's work. How do they realize that that's the thing to do, and how does that process happen?

495
01:34:29,760 --> 01:34:37,680
I guess you gave your local answer. You read these papers, and you immediately realized there's work to be done here. There's low-hanging fruit.

496
01:34:37,680 --> 01:34:48,000
There's some deep provocative idea that I need to better understand, and I could tractably make progress on. To some extent, you're saying, "Okay,

497
01:34:48,000 --> 01:35:01,840
I wanted to get into this game of contributing to humanity's understanding of the universe," and you are applying this low-hanging fruit algorithm. You're like, "elative to my particular set of

498
01:35:01,840 --> 01:35:16,880
interests and abilities, where should I pick up my shovel and start digging?" There it was like, "Oh, this looks like quite a good place to start digging."

499
01:35:16,880 --> 01:35:29,120
Different people, of course, chose very differently. It was a very unusual choice at the time. This was 1992. Very few people were thinking about that.

500
01:35:29,120 --> 01:35:41,200
Fast-forwarding a bit, I don't know how you think about your work on the open science movement now, but did it work? What does success there look like?

501
01:35:41,200 --> 01:35:58,960
What is the movement trying to accomplish? It's interesting. You didn't stop and define open science there, which 20 years ago you would have had to do. People recognize the phrase.

502
01:35:58,960 --> 01:36:06,000
People have some set of associations with it. Most often, they have a relatively simple set of associations. It means maybe something

503
01:36:06,000 --> 01:36:19,920
about making scientific papers open access. Very often they have some set of notions about also making code openly available or making data openly available.

504
01:36:19,920 --> 01:36:35,200
Those are already very large successes of the open science movement, to make those salient issues. Those are issues on which people have opinions, and there are relatively common arguments.

505
01:36:35,200 --> 01:36:47,600
This is like the meme version: publicly funded science should be open science. That's a distillation of a set of ideas which you might be able to contest.

506
01:36:47,600 --> 01:37:01,680
But if you can get people actually thinking about it and engaged with that kind of argument, that's a very fundamental issue to be considering in the whole political economy of science.

507
01:37:01,680 --> 01:37:13,040
If you go back three centuries, there was a very similar argument prosecuted, which is the question: do we publicly disclose our scientific results or not?

508
01:37:13,040 --> 01:37:26,240
If you look at people like Galileo and Kepler, the extent to which they publicly disclosed was done in a very odd way. Sometimes they did bizarre things where

509
01:37:26,240 --> 01:38:02,560
they published some of their results as anagrams. They'd find some discovery, write down the result in a sentence, scramble it, and publish that. Then if somebody else later made the same

510
01:38:02,560 --> 01:38:12,720
discovery, they would unscramble the anagram and say, "Oh, yeah, I actually did it first." This is not an ideal foundation for a discovery system.

511
01:38:12,720 --> 01:38:27,840
It took a very long time, over a century, I think, to obtain more or less the modern ideals, in which you disclose the knowledge in the form of a paper. There is an expectation of attribution, and a

512
01:38:27,840 --> 01:38:37,200
reputation economy gets built. "So-and-so did this work, so they deserve the credit for that," and that's the basis for their careers. This is the underlying

513
01:38:37,200 --> 01:38:48,160
political economy of science. That made a lot of sense when you have a printing press and the ability to do scientific journals. Then you transition to this modern situation,

514
01:38:48,160 --> 01:39:00,960
where you can start to share a lot more. You can share your code, your data, your in-progress ideas. But there's no direct credit associated to those.

515
01:39:00,960 --> 01:39:18,080
It's not at all obvious how much reputation should be associated to them. That's all constructed socially. Making it a live issue is a very important thing to have done.

516
01:39:18,080 --> 01:39:28,800
I view that as one of the main positive outcomes of work on open science. I'll give you a really practical example to illustrate the problem.

517
01:39:28,800 --> 01:39:42,400
For a long time in physics, there was a preprint culture in which people would upload preprints to the preprint archive, and in biology, this didn't happen.

518
01:39:42,400 --> 01:39:54,080
There was no preprint culture. That's changing now, but for a long time, this was the case. I used to amuse myself by asking physicists and biologists why this was the case.

519
01:39:54,080 --> 01:40:10,080
What I would hear from biologists was they would say, "Biology is so much more competitive than physics that we need to protect our priority, so we can't possibly upload to the archive.

520
01:40:10,080 --> 01:40:18,080
We have to just publish in journals." Then I would sometimes hear from physicists, "Physics is so much more competitive than biology that we need to establish

521
01:40:18,080 --> 01:40:25,280
our priority by uploading as rapidly as possible to the preprint archive. We can't possibly wait to do it with the journals."

522
01:40:25,280 --> 01:40:36,000
I think this emphasizes the extent to which this kind of attribution economy is just something we construct. It's something we do by agreement.

523
01:40:36,000 --> 01:40:51,600
Any attempt to change that economy results in a different system by which we construct knowledge. There is this very fundamental set of problems around the political economy of science.

524
01:40:51,600 --> 01:41:01,760
We've got this collective project, and how we mediate it depends upon the economy we have around ideas. One of the things you've emphasized

525
01:41:01,760 --> 01:41:11,120
as a part of this project of open science, and we talked about it earlier, is collective science, or groups of people making progress on a problem where no individual understands

526
01:41:11,120 --> 01:41:24,320
all the logical and explanatory levels necessary to make a leap or a connection. Outside of mathematics, what is the best example of such a discovery?

527
01:41:24,320 --> 01:41:34,800
I'm not sure I have a well-ordering of them to give you a best. An example that I think is very interesting is the LHC, where it's

528
01:41:34,800 --> 01:41:44,240
just this immensely complicated object. Years ago, I snuck into an accelerator physics conference. I didn't know anything at

529
01:41:44,240 --> 01:41:53,680
all about accelerator physics, but I was just curious to see what they were talking about. This particular group of people were experts on numerical methods,

530
01:41:53,680 --> 01:42:04,880
in particular on inverse methods. Inside these accelerators, you have these cascades. A particle will be massively accelerated, maybe

531
01:42:04,880 --> 01:42:17,360
it'll be collided, and then you'll get a shower of particles which decays and decays and decays. There's just this incredible, consequential shower, which is ultimately what you see

532
01:42:17,360 --> 01:42:29,280
at the detector. Then you have to retroactively figure out what produced it. There are these very complicated inverse problems that need to be solved. You've got this final data,

533
01:42:29,280 --> 01:42:40,160
but you need to figure out what produced it, and that's how you look for signatures of these. Many of these people were incredibly deep experts on simulation methods

534
01:42:40,160 --> 01:42:52,480
for following particle tracks. This was really deep and difficult stuff. I was like, "Wow, you could spend a lifetime just learning how to do this and how to solve

535
01:42:52,480 --> 01:43:09,600
some of these inverse problems, and you would know very little about quantum field theory, detector physics, vacuum physics, or data processing, all these things that are absolutely essential

536
01:43:09,600 --> 01:43:22,960
to understanding, say, the Higgs boson". I don't think it's possible for one person to understand everything in depth. Lots of people broadly understand a

537
01:43:22,960 --> 01:43:34,880
lot of these ideas, but they don't understand everything in the depth that is actually utilized. That's why there are these papers with well over a thousand authors.

538
01:43:34,880 --> 01:43:45,440
Those people can talk to one another at a high level, but they don't understand each other's specialties in all that much depth. Things like detector physics, vacuum physics,

539
01:43:45,440 --> 01:44:02,080
solving inverse problems, this stuff is incredibly different from each other. To understand it in real detail is serious work. How do you think about prolificness versus depth?

540
01:44:02,080 --> 01:44:11,280
Maybe Darwin's an example of somebody who's just gestating on something for many decades. There are other examples. Einstein during the year he comes up with special relativity is

541
01:44:11,280 --> 01:44:20,560
just doing a bunch of different things. And Pais talks about how they were all relevant to the eventual build-up. It's something I stress about a lot.

542
01:44:20,560 --> 01:44:33,040
Sometimes I feel I'm too slow. It's funny though, the Darwin example is really interesting. Prolific at what? God knows how many letters he wrote.

543
01:44:33,040 --> 01:44:46,560
It must have been an enormous number. So he was certainly very active. There's two types of work that tend to be involved in any kind of creative project.

544
01:44:46,560 --> 01:44:54,320
There's routine stuff, and there you just want to avoid procrastination. You just want to ask, "How do I get good at this?" or "How do I outsource it?" and "How do I do it as

545
01:44:54,320 --> 01:45:11,200
rapidly as possible?" and just avoid getting into a situation where you're prolonging it. Then there's high-variance stuff where you actually need to be willing to take a lot of time.

546
01:45:11,200 --> 01:45:20,720
You need to be willing to go to different places and talk to different people, where in any given instance, most of it is just not going to be an input.

547
01:45:20,720 --> 01:45:31,920
Somehow balancing those two things… I think a lot of people are very good at doing one or the other, but it's almost like a personality trait which one you prefer.

548
01:45:31,920 --> 01:45:45,040
People tend to end up doing a lot of one and not enough of the other. So I certainly try and balance those two things. Einstein is such an interesting example. 1905

549
01:45:45,040 --> 01:45:53,360
is just this extraordinary year. You can delete special relativity entirely, and it's an extraordinary year. You can delete special relativity, and you can

550
01:45:53,360 --> 01:46:07,520
delete the photoelectric effect for which he won the Nobel Prize, and it's still an extraordinary year, plausibly a multi-Nobel-Prize-winning year. So what's he doing? Maybe the answer is

551
01:46:07,520 --> 01:46:22,560
just that he's smarter than the rest of us. There's a lot of luck as well. Certainly for myself anyway, trying to identify those things that are routine

552
01:46:22,560 --> 01:46:34,560
that I should get good at, and then just try to do them as quickly as possible. I think that's yielded a certain amount of returns. But also being willing to bet a little bit more on myself on the

553
01:46:34,560 --> 01:46:45,920
variance side has also been very, very helpful. That's really hard, because intrinsically you're putting yourself in situations where you don't know what the outcome is going to be.

554
01:46:45,920 --> 01:46:58,000
If you're very driven to be productive, and actually mostly it's not working over there, you think, "Let's reduce this." It doesn't feel right. When I worked in San Francisco,

555
01:46:58,000 --> 01:47:08,000
a practice I used to have each day was instead of taking the 15-minute walk to work, I would take the more beautiful 30-minute walk.

556
01:47:08,000 --> 01:47:19,200
Partially just because it was beautiful, but partially also as just a reminder that there are real benefits to not being efficient. But it's not an answer to your question.

557
01:47:19,200 --> 01:47:30,320
Really, I think all I'm saying is I struggle a lot with the question. I think Dean Keith Simonton has this famous equal odds rule where he says

558
01:47:30,320 --> 01:47:40,480
the probability that any given thing you release—any paper, book, whatever—will be extremely important for a given person through their lifetime is not that different.

559
01:47:40,480 --> 01:47:53,360
What really determines in what era they are the most productive is how much they're publishing. Any given thing has equal odds of being extremely important.

560
01:47:53,360 --> 01:48:03,200
I think some of the most successful creatives or scientists, they're just doing a lot. Shakespeare was just publishing a lot. Of course, then there are counterexamples. Gödel

561
01:48:03,200 --> 01:48:23,680
published almost nothing. But broadly speaking, you need a very good reason to not do that. It's funny, I've met a lot of people over the years who are clearly brilliant,

562
01:48:23,680 --> 01:48:33,680
and they're just obsessed that they are going to work on the great project that makes them famous, and they never do anything. That seems connected. It's a type of aversiveness. I think very

563
01:48:33,680 --> 01:48:45,760
often they just don't want public judgment. Something that I would love to see… There's an awful lot of biographies and memoirs and histories of people who achieve a lot.

564
01:48:45,760 --> 01:49:02,960
I wish there was a very large number of biographies of people who are fantastically talented who just missed. I've known people who won gold medals at IMOs

565
01:49:02,960 --> 01:49:18,000
and things like that, who then tried to become mathematicians and failed. What happened? What was the reason? I suspect in many cases that's actually more informative than anything else.

566
01:49:18,000 --> 01:49:28,800
You have this essay that I was reading before this interview about how you think about what the work you're doing is. And "writer" doesn't seem like the right label.

567
01:49:28,800 --> 01:49:41,600
As you say, was Charles Darwin a writer? What exactly is that label? I'm a podcaster. In a way, obviously our work is very different, but I also think a lot about what this

568
01:49:41,600 --> 01:49:50,480
work is and how I get better at it. In particular, how can I make sure there's some compounding between the different people I talk to on the podcast?

569
01:49:50,480 --> 01:50:04,560
I worry that instead of this compounding, I build up some understanding that's somewhat superficial about a topic, and then it depreciates. I move down to the next topic, and it depreciates.

570
01:50:04,560 --> 01:50:15,680
There are a lot of podcasters in the world who will interview way more experts than I have, and I don't think they're much the wiser or more knowledgeable as a result.

571
01:50:15,680 --> 01:50:29,120
So it's clearly possible to mess this up. I wonder if you have thoughts or takes or advice on how one actually learns in a deeper way from this kind of work.

572
01:50:29,120 --> 01:50:42,560
It's an incredibly complicated and rich question. It seems like the question is, how do you make it a higher-growth context? How do you make it a more demanding context?

573
01:50:42,560 --> 01:50:52,880
You can do that in relatively small ways that might yield compounding returns, or you can do something that is more radical. Maybe it means starting a parallel project in

574
01:50:52,880 --> 01:51:02,160
which you do something that is actually quite a bit different. There is something really interesting about how being very demanding can

575
01:51:02,160 --> 01:51:10,880
simply change your response to something. Something that I would sometimes do with students and sometimes with myself, it was really aimed more at myself,

576
01:51:10,880 --> 01:51:23,840
was they would say some week, "I'm going to try and do this work over the coming week." Then the next week would come by and they hadn't solved the problem.

577
01:51:23,840 --> 01:51:36,240
If a million dollars had been at stake, would you have put the same effort in? And the answer is no, invariably. They've tried, but they haven't really tried.

578
01:51:36,240 --> 01:51:50,320
I think that's a very familiar feeling for all of us. You could do a lot more if you had just the right demanding taskmaster standing by you and saying,

579
01:51:50,320 --> 01:52:01,600
"Look, you're barely operating here." I do wonder a little bit about what's the demanding taskmaster? What can they ask you that is going

580
01:52:01,600 --> 01:52:14,000
to make your preparation way more intense? The most helpful thing honestly is… For some subjects it is very clear how I prep. I'm doing an upcoming episode on chip

581
01:52:14,000 --> 01:52:27,200
design with the founder of a company that does chip design, and he wrote a textbook on it. Yesterday I went over to his office, and we brainstormed five roofline analyses I can do.

582
01:52:27,200 --> 01:52:37,120
If I understand that, I have some good understanding. The problem is with almost every other field, there's not this curriculum.

583
01:52:37,120 --> 01:52:45,280
When I interviewed Ilya three, four years ago, it was: implement the transformer, and if you implement it, you have some nugget of understanding you have clamped down.

584
01:52:45,280 --> 01:52:58,560
With other fields, it's just that I vaguely understand this. It's not clamped. There's no forcing function of "do this exercise, and if you do it, you will understand."

585
01:52:58,560 --> 01:53:07,120
Really what you're saying is you can do a good job at podcasting without actually attaining this kind of understanding, and that's the problem from your point of view.

586
01:53:07,120 --> 01:53:18,000
You want to change your job description so that you are internalizing these chunks and just getting this kind of integration each time. It seems to me that what that means is you

587
01:53:18,000 --> 01:53:34,160
actually want to change the structure of the work output at some level. There’s this terrible idea that lots of people have that they should be in flow all of the time.

588
01:53:34,160 --> 01:53:41,920
And as far as I can tell, high performers just don't believe this at all. They're in flow some of the time. You certainly see this with athletes.

589
01:53:41,920 --> 01:53:51,200
When they're actually out there playing basketball or tennis, ideally they are in flow much of the time. But when they're training they're not.

590
01:53:51,200 --> 01:54:00,400
They're stuck a lot of the time, or they're doing things badly. I suppose I wonder what that looks like for you. That I would be extremely satisfied with.

591
01:54:00,400 --> 01:54:12,960
The problem is I just don't know what the equivalent of doing 64 laps is. This is a thing you can change by choosing guests where there is a legible curriculum.

592
01:54:12,960 --> 01:54:28,720
So maybe it's a mistake not to have done that. Also, there's no real way to prep for Terence Tao. There's no curriculum that's a plausible one. There are many failure modes, but one

593
01:54:28,720 --> 01:54:39,680
long-term dynamic I'm worried about is that you can have a good podcast and reach a local maximum, but for no particular guest or topic are you going deep enough.

594
01:54:39,680 --> 01:54:49,280
My model of learning is that if you don't really understand the deeper mechanism, you're just mapping inputs and outputs of a black box. That just fades incredibly fast or is

595
01:54:49,280 --> 01:55:05,680
not worth it in the first place. You just move on and it's over. You need to build the intermediate connection. AI in a weird way is really easy for that reason,

596
01:55:05,680 --> 01:55:16,880
because there is a clear thing you can do. Just implement it, and then you understand it. If I applied that criterion elsewhere, do I just not do history episodes?

597
01:55:16,880 --> 01:55:27,680
Exactly. Ada Palmer. Wonderful to talk to, incredibly interesting. But for you personally, what changed? There are some things I learned.

598
01:55:27,680 --> 01:55:36,960
If I had allocated more time, especially after the interview, to write up 2,000 words on everything I learned and how it connects to other things I know.

599
01:55:36,960 --> 01:55:46,880
Maybe that's a thing worth doing, spreading out the episodes more and spending more time afterwards consolidating. I would pay infinite amounts of money if there was

600
01:55:46,880 --> 01:55:55,920
somebody who was really good at coming up with the curriculum, the practice problems you need to do, and the exercise you need to do after the interview to clamp what you have learned.

601
01:55:55,920 --> 01:56:04,480
Have you tried doing that with somebody? It's hard to find someone. I haven't tried super hard, but isn't it going to be tough to find somebody who could do that

602
01:56:04,480 --> 01:56:12,400
for every single kind of discipline? Maybe I should just hire different ones for different topics. Maybe. There's something about,

603
01:56:12,400 --> 01:56:24,560
what problem are you solving for each episode? As far as I can tell, that's the only way I really understand anything. I get interested in something. At first, I don't even have a problem,

604
01:56:24,560 --> 01:56:32,560
but there's just some sense that there's some contribution to make here, and gradually you hone in, and there's a problem. Funnily enough, spending time

605
01:56:32,560 --> 01:56:47,760
stuck is incredibly important. That used to just be annoying. Now it seems like it's maybe even the most important part of the whole process.

606
01:56:47,760 --> 01:57:01,680
That hard-won nature of it means that I internalize it afterwards. I've written 10,000-word essays in a couple of days, and I've written

607
01:57:01,680 --> 01:57:16,240
them in three months or six months. I feel like I didn't learn very much from the ones that only took a couple of days. Whereas some of the ones that took three months,

608
01:57:16,240 --> 01:57:31,280
15 years later, I'll still remember. Can you describe outside of physics how you learn, of the ones that took three months? By far the most common thing is there's always

609
01:57:31,280 --> 01:57:45,200
some creative artifact. Sometimes it's a class. Sometimes it's engagement with a group of people who are working on some collective creative artifact together.

610
01:57:45,200 --> 01:57:56,640
You might not even be aware of it, but you're acting as an input to their creative ends in some way. Sometimes it's an essay or a book or whatever.

611
01:57:56,640 --> 01:58:10,240
It's one of the reasons why I often quite enjoy doing podcasts. I said yes to come here partially because I know you ask unusually demanding questions.

612
01:58:10,240 --> 01:58:20,080
That's an attempt to get this sort of perspective from a different kind of a forcing function. Trying to pick the most demanding creative context.

613
01:58:20,080 --> 01:58:27,680
For this interview, I went through three lectures of the Susskind special relativity book. The problem is that there's almost no practice problems in it.

614
01:58:27,680 --> 01:58:39,760
So I hired a physicist friend. I haven't done it yet, but for every lecture I want a bunch of practice problems to go through, and I'm planning on being appropriately humbled.

615
01:58:39,760 --> 01:58:49,120
How do you make it as jugular as possible? The higher you can raise the stakes, the better. The interview is in some sense high stakes, but also

616
01:58:49,120 --> 01:58:57,760
it doesn't necessarily test deep understanding. I don't think the interview is that high stakes. You're not writing a book about special relativity, and you're not trying to write a

617
01:58:57,760 --> 01:59:16,240
book that replaces whatever the existing standard textbook is. That's a really high stake. By the way, a phrase that I find particularly difficult. People will talk about "going deep" on a subject,

618
01:59:16,240 --> 01:59:24,600
and it turns out different people have different ideas of what this means. For some people it means they read a couple of blog posts. For some people it means they read a book about it.

619
01:59:24,600 --> 01:59:36,000
For some people it means they wrote a book about it. The standard you hold yourself to determines a lot about your

620
01:59:36,000 --> 01:59:51,840
ability to integrate knowledge in this way. I found that I'm in some sense able to move much faster on some things through the help of AI, but I don't know if I'm learning better.

621
01:59:51,840 --> 02:00:00,480
I think it's probably because… The hardest thing, the thing that is most demanding, is so aversive that you try to take any excuse you can to get out of it.

622
02:00:00,480 --> 02:00:07,680
Just having a back-and-forth conversation with an LLM where you gloss over… It’s entertaining but not necessarily anything else.

623
02:00:07,680 --> 02:00:17,280
It’s such an easy way to get out of the thing. In fact, it makes it easier because instead of doing some intermediate thinking, there's always a next question you can ask a chatbot.

624
02:00:17,280 --> 02:00:33,840
Yeah. And it's somewhat valuable. That’s part of the seductiveness, of course. It's not actually useless. But it can substitute for actually doing the thing that maybe you should be doing.

625
02:00:33,840 --> 02:00:55,920
It’s interesting. To what extent should you be outsourcing that kind of stuff? It’s an interesting judgment call. There is a whole bunch of routine work that you want done.

626
02:00:55,920 --> 02:01:04,640
It's low value for you, so if you can get a chatbot to do it, you may as well. Somebody interviewed the pioneering computer scientist Alan Kay years ago,

627
02:01:04,640 --> 02:01:13,360
and he was asked what he thought about Linux. If I remember his answer correctly, he basically said, "It doesn't have anything to do with computer science.

628
02:01:13,360 --> 02:01:24,800
It's just a great big ball of mud. There are a few interesting ideas in there which are worth understanding, but mostly all you're learning is stuff about Linux.

629
02:01:24,800 --> 02:01:38,160
You're not actually learning anything which is transferable." I thought that was very interesting. There's a certain kind of seductiveness to some things where it's sort of a Rube Goldberg machine.

630
02:01:38,160 --> 02:01:48,400
You can just learn about all the bits, and it feels entertaining. But if you step back and think about what you're actually doing here,

631
02:01:48,400 --> 02:01:57,360
it might not actually be meeting your objectives. Maybe you want to become a sysadmin, and learning Linux is a great use of your time. There's no harm in that at all.

632
02:01:57,360 --> 02:02:08,400
But if your objective is to understand the fundamentals of computing, it's much less clear that that's a good use of your time. It was certainly an answer I've thought a

633
02:02:08,400 --> 02:02:24,240
lot about, where for a certain type of mind, there is a seductiveness in just learning systems and confusing that with understanding. Okay, I'll keep you updated on how this goes.

634
02:02:24,240 --> 02:02:33,120
I owe you a text within a month of some revamped learning system. I'd be really curious. It's also true that tiny incremental improvements

635
02:02:33,120 --> 02:02:43,040
in this are just worth so much. It's the main input into the podcast. It's great that the bookshelves are fancy and I've got a blackboard or whatever,

636
02:02:43,040 --> 02:02:58,400
but really the thing that makes the podcast better is if I can improve the learning I do. So yes, it's worth every morsel of improvement. All right, thanks for the therapy session. Great

637
02:02:58,400 --> 02:03:02,964
note to end on. Thanks, Michael. All right. Thanks, Dwarkesh.
